  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern continues to be dominated by a large upper-\nlevel high over the southeastern CONUS. Nearer to the surface, RAP \nmesoanalysis has a weak low pressure system in eastern Colorado. \nThis is helped provide the dynamics for weak thunderstorms in \neastern Colorado/western Kansas this afternoon and evening. The \ncooler air from these storms will be short-lived as temperatures \nrace back up into the upper 90s and into the triple digits \nWednesday. Similar today, a few weak pop-up thunderstorms during \npeak heating in the afternoon across the western zones. With very \nlittle shear and CAPE in forecast soundings (<20 KTs and <1000 J/kg \nrespectively), these storms are not anticipated to approach severe \nthresholds. Modest lapse rates may be supportive of gusts up to 40 \nmph in the strongest storms that form, but lightning will be the \nprimary hazard. CAMs have varying solutions remaining mostly dry, \nbut have mostly underrepresented convection in this pattern. That \nsaid, there may not be enough ingredients to overcome the CAP and \nremain clear. The forecasted probability of precipitation remains \nbelow 10% for any specific area. \n\nThursday is forecast to be a near repeat of Wednesday. Highs near \nand passed the 100 degree mark along with a slim chance (<10%) for \nweak convection in very far SW Kansas. Again in the event convection \nis able to materialize, it is forecast to be weak and sub-severe \nwith very limited coverage. Friday and Saturday will continue the \nrepetition in pattern with highs near 100 degrees with low chances \n(10-20%) for precipitation in the far western zones. Time will need to \npass to get a better grasp on how they will materialize if at all. \nLooking far ahead at long-ranged ensembles, a more widespread \nprecipitation event is hinted at next Wednesday with highs cooler in \nthe low/mid 90s. Especially for the eastern counties, this is the \nnext best chance for some drought relief.",
